// @run
// @log 3
// RETIRED AS A REJECT FLOOR — THE FILE KEEPS ITS NAME AND ITS PROGRAM, AND NOW PINS THE
// ANSWER INSTEAD OF THE REFUSAL. It was an `@emit-error` fixture holding
// `shapeElemDeclaredStructIdx`'s trailing `nameIsStructDecl` gate closed. The gate's own
// header said the reject had to hold because admitting an inline spelling that resolves
// only to an anonymous / interned-shape row would route a shape-armed union
// (`t is {f: boolean}[]` over an inferred closure-call binding) into the union-arm narrow
// machinery, whose read side mis-lowers that flow.
//
// THAT PREMISE WAS REFUTED BY MEASUREMENT, TWICE, AND THE SECOND TIME CONTRADICTED THE
// FIRST'S REASON. #2001 recorded the harm as "an emit reject on both sides, because with
// no declared twin the gate never fires". Re-measured for #2004 the cell is not an emit
// reject on either side: it RUNS, printing the right value, on base and on the lift. The
// conclusion survives and the stated reason does not, so here is the evidence rather than
// the sentence:
//
//   · A POISON PROBE at the gate, over the 2,386 corpus modules that predate this change,
//     fires on THREE — the two
//     cells whose resolved row really is anonymous
//     (`closures/closure-result-array-of-map-closure-field-structs.vl` at `#anon0`,
//     `closures/closure-result-value-union-field-nested-composite.vl` at `#anon2`) and
//     this file. Both anonymous ones build BYTE-IDENTICALLY with the gate lifted, so the
//     reach is live and the answer does not move.
//   · A 10-CELL GRID of the protected population — the shape-armed union narrowed from an
//     inferred closure call, from an annotated one, at a param / a global / a return, both
//     arms taken, plus the same-fieldset NESTED twin the gate's other header names — moves
//     0 cells and is correct on both sides.
//   · The whole corpus is byte-identical (2,391 modules, 1,948 identical, 0 DIFFER,
//     0 LOST) and the
//     distilled corpus loses 0 `runs` and moves 0 classes to silent.
//
// A PIN WHOSE PREMISE IS REFUTED IS A PIN TO RETIRE WITH ITS EVIDENCE, so the reject is
// gone and this program is now graded on the value it produces. What the original note got
// RIGHT is worth keeping: the two predicates are different questions, and `sRowDecl` (which
// banks `cUserTypes.has(nm)`, unions included) is still the wrong column for a caller that
// wants "is this a declared STRUCT". The gate did not go away by being swapped for it — it
// went away because "is this a declared struct" turned out not to be a question this caller
// needs to ask. See `silent-class-inventory.md` D402, and `types/intersection-elem-permuted-
// arm-field-alias.vl` for the field-order rule the same landing had to add.
type S = {a: i32} & {b: i32}
function mk(): {a: i32, b: i32}[] { return [{ a: 1, b: 2 }] }
const xs = mk()
print(xs[0].a + xs[0].b)
